<h1>why do companies say entry level then reject me for not having 2 years experience</h1>

<p class="answer-capsule">"Entry level" is a recruiting theater term, not a promise. Companies post it to maximize applicant volume, then filter for candidates who already paid the experience tax elsewhere through internships, adjacent roles, or hidden prerequisites they never listed.</p>

<div class="tldr">
  <ul>
    <li>Only <a href="https://www.naceweb.org/">31% of new-grad hires come through formal campus recruiting</a>, meaning most "entry level" roles are filled by candidates who already have some work history.</li>
    <li><a href="https://www.burningglassinstitute.org/research/skills-based-hiring-2024">Fewer than 1 in 700 hires reflected real degree-requirement change</a> even after 46% of employers claimed to drop them, showing how little job postings match actual hiring behavior.</li>
    <li><a href="https://cdn.prod.website-files.com/6777c52f82e5471a3732ea25/679a6fadfda4220bbac585d7_Talent-Disrupted-2.pdf">An internship cuts underemployment odds by 48.5%</a>, which is why candidates with that hidden credential beat you for roles labeled "no experience needed."</li>
    <li>New-grad hiring at big tech is <a href="https://www.signalfire.com/blog/signalfire-state-of-talent-report-2025">down more than 50% from 2019</a>, so employers can demand 2 years for entry-level seats that used to go to true beginners.</li>
    <li>The real filter is not the job description, it is proof of work: scored demonstrations that shortcut the experience guessing game entirely.</li>
  </ul>
</div>

<section>
  <h2>what "entry level" actually means in recruiting software</h2>
  <p>Job postings are written for applicant tracking systems, not humans. "Entry level" is a keyword bucket that pulls in the widest possible search pool. Recruiters know that someone with two years of internship or contract work will apply alongside true beginners, and the system, or the human scanning resumes, will sort accordingly.</p>
  
  <p>The experience requirement is often invisible: "preferred" qualifications buried below "required" ones, or cultural code for "we want someone who has already been trained by another employer." Companies externalize the cost of onboarding onto your previous roles, internships, or side projects. They call it entry level because the <em>pay</em> is entry level, not because the <em>profile</em> is.</p>
  
  <p>This is the filter OS at work in the job market, where the posting functions as a funnel top designed to collect volume and let the actual filters, resume screens, keyword matches, and referral preferences, do the real selection. Your rejection for lacking two years of experience is not a contradiction but the system functioning as intended.</p>
</section>

<section>
  <h2>where the "2 years" really comes from</h2>
  <p>Most of the time the hiring manager did not write that job description. A recruiter or talent operations team used a template, copied from a similar role, or let an AI draft generate "1-2 years preferred" as a default risk hedge. The number is arbitrary but the effect is real: it signals that the company wants someone who has already survived a professional environment and learned the baseline norms elsewhere.</p>
  
  <p>The two-year threshold also maps onto internship cycles. A candidate with two summers plus a part-time semester role has roughly that duration, packaged in short bursts. They read as "entry level" on paper but arrive pre-socialized. You are competing against people who gamed the experience label by fragmenting their work history across multiple short engagements, not against some mythical candidate with zero exposure.</p>
  
  <p>Meanwhile, the actual skills required for many of these roles are learnable in weeks, and the experience ask is a proxy for "can you navigate ambiguity, communicate with stakeholders, ship something real," all things that can be demonstrated without a two-year employment record. But employers lack the infrastructure to evaluate that directly, so they default to the crude signal of tenure.</p>
</section>

<section>
  <h2>why the gap keeps widening</h2>
  <p>The pipeline from university to job is structurally broken, and both sides know it. Companies stopped investing in new-grad training programs because the market lets them hire someone who already did that training at a previous company or internship. Big tech, which once absorbed thousands of new graduates, has <a href="https://www.signalfire.com/blog/signalfire-state-of-talent-report-2025">cut new-grad hiring by more than half since 2019</a>. When supply of "slightly experienced" candidates exceeds demand for true beginners, the floor rises.</p>
  
  <p>Students feel this as a betrayal: you followed the rules, got the degree, and now the door requires a credential that was never part of the advertised path. The honest frame is that the degree was never sufficient, it was just the filter that let you compete, while the real credential, increasingly, is proof that you have already done the work in a context that resembles employment.</p>
  
  <p>This is where the degree-as-mortgage analogy bites hardest: you borrowed against future earnings to buy an asset that depreciated faster than your payments, and the "entry level" label on job postings is the marketing for that asset, promising access that the actual hiring process does not deliver.</p>
</section>

<section>
  <h2>what actually breaks through the experience wall</h2>
  <p>Recruiters are not evil, they are overwhelmed. A typical corporate role draws hundreds of applications, and experience is the fastest triage mechanism. The way around it is not to complain about hypocrisy but to become the candidate who does not need the experience proxy because you bring a stronger signal.</p>
  
  <p>That signal is scored proof of work: demonstrations that you can do the specific tasks the role requires, evaluated against a professional bar. Not a certificate that says you completed a course. Not a badge nobody opens. Actual submissions, with feedback, that show how you think, iterate, and execute. This is what the <a href="https://www.testgorilla.com/skills-based-hiring/state-of-skills-based-hiring-2025/">85% of employers using skills-based hiring</a> are theoretically looking for, though most still lack systems to collect it at scale.</p>
  
  <p>The candidates who bypass the two-year requirement do it through one of three paths: a referral that overrides the resume screen, an internship that counts as the experience in disguise, or a portfolio of work so specific to the role that the hiring manager stops counting years and starts evaluating output. The third path is the only one fully in your control, and it is the one zero is built for.</p>
</section>

<section>
  <h2>how to read job postings like a recruiter</h2>
  <p>Stop treating the description as a checklist of your worthiness. It is a wish list written by committee. The "required" section is often negotiable if you can substitute proof. The "preferred" section is where the real filtering happens. Two years of experience in "preferred" means the role will go to someone who has it, unless you can nullify that advantage with demonstrated capability.</p>
  
  <p>Reverse-engineer the actual work: what would this person do in month one, month three, month six? Find or create the closest possible simulation. Submit work that matches those tasks, scored, with feedback that shows iteration. When a recruiter sees proof that you have already done the core function, the years become irrelevant, but when they see only a degree and a resume, the years become the only thing they have to judge.</p>
  
  <p>The brutal truth: companies say "entry level" because it works for them, not because it means anything for you. Your move is to make the label obsolete by bringing evidence that outperforms the proxy.</p>
</section>

<section class="faq">
  <h2>frequently asked questions</h2>
  
  <div class="faq-item">
    <h3>is it worth applying to jobs that ask for 2 years when i have none</h3>
    <p>Yes, but strategically. Apply only where you can simultaneously submit proof of work that matches the role's core tasks. A naked resume against an experience filter is a waste. A resume plus scored demonstrations that nullify the experience gap is how you flip the equation.</p>
  </div>
  
  <div class="faq-item">
    <h3>why do companies get away with false advertising on experience requirements</h3>
    <p>There is no enforcement mechanism. Job postings are not contracts. The "entry level" label is optimized for search visibility and applicant volume, not accuracy. Companies face no penalty for mismatch between posting and practice.</p>
  </div>
  
  <div class="faq-item">
    <h3>do internships really count as the 2 years of experience</h3>
    <p>Often yes, especially multiple internships or those with project ownership. <a href="https://cdn.prod.website-files.com/6777c52f82e5471a3732ea25/679a6fadfda4220bbac585d7_Talent-Disrupted-2.pdf">Internship experience cuts underemployment risk by nearly half</a>, which is why candidates with that history dominate entry-level pools. The market treats internships as experience because they are experience, just cheaper for the employer who provided them.</p>
  </div>
  
  <div class="faq-item">
    <h3>what is the fastest way to get that first proof of work</h3>
    <p>Stop waiting for permission. Pick a real task from a job posting you want, do it with the tools and standards that role would use, and submit it for scored feedback against a professional bar. One strong submission beats a year of waiting for someone to give you a title.</p>
  </div>
  
  <div class="faq-item">
    <h3>will skills-based hiring actually fix this</h3>
    <p>Not on its own. <a href="https://www.burningglassinstitute.org/research/skills-based-hiring-2024">46% of employers dropped degree requirements but only 31% followed through with real change</a>, and fewer than 1 in 700 hires showed actual skills-based practice. The shift requires infrastructure: ways to collect, score, and trust proof of work at scale. Without that, the old proxies persist.</p>
  </div>
</section>

